nd i need to  get all the footage for that in one day  so i can edit it  throughout the rest of the week which  means i'm from training for like two to  three hours and filming it  and not only filming it filming it from  all different angles and doing a weird  number of sets and reps because i'm just  trying to do enough that i can get it on  film  or there's some shot i particularly want  to get like me doing a handspring into a  push-up so i try and try and try like  you guys see it once  but i need to get the right angle i need  to make it look clean so i repeat these  movements so i'm  training around that a lot of the time  this has actually been a really say  weird  aspect of my training i do these big  blocks of workouts that are just  exhausting  and then little bits of sporadic  training throughout the week when i'm  not doing that when i'm working on a  video that i can use old footage or when  i'm working on a video that is about the  brain or something  or when i'm just taking a deload week or  whatever i will  focus on training that's it's really  very similar to not to plug my book but  the  training program in super functional  training the last one  basically i use a combination of  different exercises that do multiple  things for the body all at once  you know why do pull-ups when you can  climb a rope and get  grip benefits and core benefits that are  more profound at the same time you know  that's my kind of approach  i do heavy weights and technical  movements at the start i do lighter  weights  simple isolation movements with high  repetitions at the end i do that  both through the workout and within a  single set because i like to use  mechanical drop sets  i use heavy weights so i'm training max  strength and training technique or i use  you know  a complicated technique like a handstand  push-up and then i move on to the much  easier variation like push-ups and just  bang them out for high repetitions so  i'm getting the conditioning benefit